Notes: The original destructive 31-byte unsupported file SHA-256 was 1d5a43857fefdd41d3ff766bb0483181cc89c08e1c3f99b5db692f757447c0f8. The host calculated size=31, padded transfer_size=35, frame_count=5, and crc32=D89550E7. The BMS returned START_ACK for image_size=35/frame_count=5, DATA_ACK for all 5 frames, and an all-FF FINISH_ACK. The old tool therefore returned 0 and falsely reported Update successful for an incompatible image. The BMS then hung and required external 485 reflashing. Both host-side preflight validation and BMS-side image validation failed to prevent activation. Follow-up fix: the merged and standalone update tools now require raw firmware filenames and zip-contained firmware payload filenames to start with uppercase BSL. For the safe GX regression, OTHER-BMS-unsupported.bin was replaced with a byte-identical copy of valid BSL-V1.245.bin; both files had SHA-256 8a7efc20671a1bbc5d6a0cf71c6141c6ba6e3a48a2fb61860b1c3cec6bff3f7e. The updated GX tool returned Firmware is not compatible with code 5 and did not create a CAN log, proving rejection occurred before CAN was opened. Filename filtering reduces immediate risk but does not replace validation of official firmware magic, target model, hardware revision, supported version path, payload length, CRC/signature, or post-update device verification.
